# Code owners — who must approve a change to the files below.
#
# GitHub enforces this only where branch protection requires code-owner review;
# without that it is an automatic reviewer request. Either way the point is the
# same: these paths must not be changed by one person alone.

# ── Prompt artifacts (engineering conventions §16) ───────────────
# A prompt edit changes what a machine-written suspicious-activity report says
# about people's money, with no type error, no failing test and no deploy
# artifact that looks different. The MANIFEST is what turns such an edit into a
# reviewable hunk; both it and the artifacts it pins are owned here.
/crates/*/prompts/            @KyawKyawThar
/crates/llm/src/prompt.rs     @KyawKyawThar

# ── The §20.4 safety boundaries themselves ───────────────────────
# The citation check, its after-the-fact audit, and the per-kind boundary
# registry are the mechanisms the "an LLM output is a proposal, never a fact"
# argument rests on. Weakening one is a governance decision, not a refactor.
/crates/copilot/src/grounding.rs        @KyawKyawThar
/crates/copilot/src/grounding_audit.rs  @KyawKyawThar
/crates/copilot/src/capability.rs       @KyawKyawThar
